{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Plan-review PASSED and certified (head_sha 31a37c1223411a836edabdea11d87d839264cd74, currency_basis file_impact) after restructuring the plan to the readiness floor. The prior run BLOCKED on two legitimate findings: the ticket was filed with prose bullets rather than a `- [ ]` checklist under `## Acceptance Criteria`, and it named no proving commands — so nothing in it was checkable.\n\n## An acknowledged gap the gate caught, recorded here rather than silently dropped\n\nAdvisory T5b/E1: **root cause item 2 — nginx's `upstream rebar_mcp` is a single `server` line with no `max_fails`, `fail_timeout`, `backup` or `proxy_next_upstream` — is named as a confirmed root cause, but no acceptance criterion remediates it and it is not listed among the deliberate exclusions either.** That is accurate and it was my omission: I documented the mechanism and then left it out of the plan in both directions.\n\nStating the disposition explicitly now. It is **deferred, not fixed**, for these reasons:\n\n- It is a change to the nginx serving TOPOLOGY, not to the capacity question this ticket's remaining scope covers. Adding failover means deciding what the second backend IS — during a blue-green cutover the old container is briefly still alive, but in steady state there is exactly one, so 'fail over to what?' has no answer today without also changing how many MCP backends run.\n- With the retire-sweep fix (change 2567, merged) plus `restart: always` and the container healthcheck, a crashed backend is now a ~30s blip rather than permanent. Failover would shrink that further; it is no longer the difference between an outage and no outage.\n- The genuine end-state for this — running MCP with more than one backend, or moving gate execution off-box entirely — is the epic-sized work already identified for AC5's data to inform.\n\nI am NOT adding it as an AC to this ticket, because doing so would commit this bug to a topology change the operator has not scoped. Recording it here so it is a decision rather than an oversight, and so the next reader does not have to rediscover that the single-server upstream was known and left.\n\nThe other two advisories are non-blocking and noted: AC4 asserts new measurement capability without naming its own novelty explicitly (T3), and a general definition-of-done quality note (P6). Neither changes what the work is."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788465831155325001,"uuid":"8a7826e7-2a07-4fe5-9942-8c7f811a1f63"}